===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{settheory}} A [[finite]] [[sequence]] of [[term]]s; equivalently, an [[ordered]] [[set]].
# {{computing}} A single [[row]] in a [[relational database]].
# {{computing}} A [[set]] of comma-separated [[value]]s passed to a program or operating system as a [[parameter]] to a [[function call]].
# {{colloqially}} A pair of things taken together as a unit, where the order of the things may or may not be important.  "[[A couple]]".
# In [[Sanscrit]] and in a few other [[ancient]] [[languages]], the [[meaning]] of any [[two]] [[words]] [[combined]] [[into]] [[one]] ''[[single]]'' [[word]] is (at least [[in principle]] [[supposed]] to be) ''[[exactly]]'' the ''[[combined]]'' [[meaning]] of the [[two]] [[words]] [[taken]] [[separately]].  In [[Sanscrit]], the [[ordering]] of the [[elements]] of a '''tuple''' ''IS'' [[important]]:  the [[ordering]] of the [[two]] [[words]] [[changes]] the [[meaning]] of the [[combined]] [[word]].

====Synonyms====
*(''in set theory''): [[n-tuple|''n''-tuple]] (''when the sequence contains'' n ''terms''), [[ordered pair]] (''when the sequence contains exactly two terms''), [[triple]] ''or'' [[triplet]] (''when the sequence contains exactly three terms'')

